This is what an intelligent SQL Assistant would do...

# The Meta-Objects or Physics of SQL

The following ideas came up discussing with Norm an agent actually learning SQL.

## The SQL Syntax and Semantics

The *syntax* of SQL does not encode the *"physics"* of querying or updating a database.

For example, here are several relevant spatial and temporal properties:

1. Processing data already in memory is faster that pulling data from disk.
1. Historical data (data before some recent time) can be stored in a manner or in a format that makes the needed queries as fast as possible.
1. New (or recent) data probably needs to be processed during a query if it has not been formatted or stored in an efficient way yet, which takes more time.

The *semantics* of SQL, for example, what an "INNER JOIN" means is an important (indeed critical) aspect of *using* SQL effectively, but it also does not express the *"physics"* above, by design really, separating the declaration of data definition or query from its implementation.

## Learning SQL

To *effectively* learn SQL requires learning the *"physics"* of SQL much more than learning the syntax or even semantics. We know this because a human can think through what they want to achieve and then experiment with an SQL statement until they get the result they want.

So the environment in which the SQL Agent learns needs to express the *"physics"* of SQL in a way that is independent of the agent's function and also provides a responsive check (e.g. like gravity to a child learning how blocks stack).

## Problem-solving in SQL

Norm related some heuristics he uses when approaching a problem with an SQL query. For example, rather than looking at the query, asking the people using it, "What does this query do?"

We can make a list of these heuristics, but we would expect an actually intelligent agent (or one capable of actual learning) to start to derive these heuristics from observing the *"physics"* of SQL.

## Communicating with an Intelligent SQL Agent

Given that the concepts involved in SQL are only superficially related to what an intelligent agent would consider about a database, Norm suggested an analogy with the whistles and chirps dolphins make. Rather than the sound patterns themselves representing language in the humans use them, what if a dolphin makes sounds in a sonar-manner that mimic the "scene" they would see if using those sounds, and the other dolphin "sees" the same scene or it evokes a similar scene, perhaps along the lines of the "mirror neurons" mechanism.

## The Economic Value of an SQL Agent

An SQL Agent of the sort described here would be able to continue to learn contextual information relevant to a particular business from a baseline of SQL competency. The agent would be a domain-specific assistant to someone who understood or was responsible for greater business scope but the agent would assist them in optimizing or evolving the business (or division, etc.) database system.
